Current Patch Set: 1&lt;br/&gt;
Commit: 958611c8d0be5992797d7028c15959876d4dbb74&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="498304" author="adilger" created="Tue, 21 Jul 2026 23:14:32 +0000">&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?name=jinshanx" class="user-hover" rel="jinshanx"&gt;jinshanx&lt;/a&gt; there are potentially already some tools that could be used here instead of adding new per-FID stats:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul class="alternate" type="square"&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;the &lt;tt&gt;lljobstat(8)&lt;/tt&gt; command is in all recent releases will generate "top-like" output for jobs generating RPCs on the local server to identify which jobs are creating a high load on the server (a distributed version is at &lt;a href="https://github.com/DDNeu/global-lustre-jobstats" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"&gt;https://github.com/DDNeu/global-lustre-jobstats&lt;/a&gt; as well).  This is usually enough to identify the problematic application, though it won't tell you which files/objects are being used.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;the &lt;tt&gt;ofd_access_log_reader&lt;/tt&gt; (ALR) utility already exists to provide per-object IO information.  It uses a lockless kernel ring buffer (producer consumer model) that logs reads/writes to all OST objects with start/end offset, bytes, segment count, timestamp. it could be used to generate "heat" or "top" metrics for OST objects with aggregation, or potentially reformatted so that it can be streamed into other visualization tools.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;I'm not against adding "heat" stats directly to the OFD and MDD, but this also adds potential issues with persistent memory usage, stats cleanup, etc.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="498316" author="JIRAUSER19463" created="Wed, 22 Jul 2026 05:59:47 +0000">&lt;p&gt;`ofd_access_log_reader` is probably what we are looking for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the information, I will check that out.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="498540" author="jinshan" created="Sun, 26 Jul 2026 01:05:34 +0000">&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;ofd_access_log_reader&lt;/tt&gt; seems to be good and it's able to get all access log. Close it.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
